class:: BlipFrac
summary:: Blip with a sample-continuous fractional harmonic count
categories:: UGens>Generators>Deterministic
related:: Guides/BlipExtensions, Classes/BlipAD, Classes/BlipDecay, Classes/BlipDiff, Classes/BlipDisp, Classes/BlipFB, Classes/BlipInt, Classes/BlipKernel, Classes/BlipPM, Classes/BlipPeak, Classes/BlipPower, Classes/BlipSeries, Classes/BlipWidth

description::

BlipFrac evaluates all harmonics below code::numharm:: and fades the next
partial by the fractional remainder. Integer values match the project's
rectangular kernel, while continuous sweeps avoid block-rate topology steps.

The harmonic request and the Nyquist boundary are both sample continuous.
During the boundary fade, a diminishing partial may lie just above the safety
frequency, so the transition is smoother rather than mathematically
brick-wall bandlimited. Zero frequency holds phase.

The closest vanilla UGen is link::Classes/Blip::. Blip uses a discrete
control-rate harmonic count; BlipFrac gives the final partial a continuous
amplitude.

classmethods::

method:: ar

argument:: freq
Frequency in Hz. Audio or control rate; signed values are valid.

argument:: numharm
Audio- or control-rate fractional count, clipped to code::1..2048:: and then
limited against Nyquist.

argument:: mul
Post-processing multiplier.

argument:: add
Post-processing offset.

examples::

code::
(
{ BlipFrac.ar(100, SinOsc.ar(0.1).range(1, 80), 0.1) }.play;
)
::

code::
(
{ BlipFrac.ar(200, LFTri.ar(1).range(1, 20), 0.1) }.play;
)
::

code::
// Modulating all parameters for a dynamic sound
(
{ BlipFrac.ar(LFNoise1.kr(2).exprange(50, 1000), MouseX.kr(1.0, 50.0)) ! 2 * 0.1 }.play;
)
::
